    "lawCode": "Amd §4310, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5973\n\nSPONSOR: HINCHEY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to allowing donate\nlife registration on electronic application forms administered by the\noffice of temporary disability assistance, the higher education services\ncorporation, and on mandatory electronic tax filings\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to provide greater opportunities for people\nto become organ donors. This bill requires the inclusion of space for\nregistering in the donate life registry for organ, eye, and tissue\ndonations by: the New York State Higher Education Services Corporation\non applications and renewals/recertifications for student aid, grants,\nscholarships, end loan programs; the Office of Temporary and Disability\nAssistance through certain websites; and by the Department of Taxation\nand Finance on certain tax documents subject to electronic filing.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends paragraph a of subdivision 5 of section 4310 of the\npublic health law, and adds the following to the approved opportunities\nto make an anatomical gift: student financial aid transactions adminis-\ntered by the Higher Education Services Corporation, certain electronic\ntax filings administered by the Department of Taxation and Finance, and\ntransactions administered through certain websites administered by the\nOffice of Temporary Disability Assistance.\n\nSection 2 amends paragraph b of subdivision 5 of section 4310 of the\npublic health law, and makes conforming changes to provide that the\nCommissioner (f. Health will consult with state agency leadership to\nimplement changes required for the operationalization of the amendments\nin Section 1, as well as any future amendments to subdivision 5 of\nsection 4310 of the public health law.\n\nSection 3 sets, the effective date of his act.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nWhile important recent improvements have been made, New York unfortu-\nnately continues to struggle regarding the percentage of residents that\nare registered as organ and tissue donors. Nearly 9,000 individuals need\na life- saving organ transplant at any given time, which represents one\nof the highest needs for donors in the United States. Over 1,500 people\nin New York State have been on the national transplant waiting list for\nmore than five years. Sadly, in 2020 alone, nearly 600 New Yorkers died\nin part due to a pronounced shortage of donors.\n\nThis legislation takes a commonsense approach to increasing opportu-\nnities for New Yorkers to register as organ and tissue donors through he\ndonate life registry. Where state government forms presenting the public\nwith the option to register as a donor are currently limited to iden-\ntification applications at the Department of Motor Vehicles, voter\nregistration forms, the state's healthcare exchange, and standalone\nforms to participate in the registry, much more can and should be done\nto encourage registration.\nThis bill mandates that additional state-issued transactions must pres-\nent New Yorkers with the opportunity to register as an organ and tissue\ndonor, including: electronically processed tax filings, applications and\nrenewals/recertifications for student aid and loan programs provided by\n:he New York State Higher Education Services Corporation, and benefits\ntransactions through certain websites operated by the Office of Tempo-\nrary and Disability Assistance.\n\nThe legislation will provide millions of New Yorkers from demograph-\nically diverse segments of the population with new and regular opportu-\nnities to document their decision to make an anatomical gift upon their\npassing. Many of the transactions being proposed are completed on an\nannual basis, which will also increase the frequency of engagement on\nthis vital topic with New Yorkers.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the one hundred eightieth day after having\nbecome a law; provided, however, that if chapter 149 of the laws of 2020\nhave not taken effect on or before such date, then this act shall take\neffect on the same date and in the same manner s such chapter of the\nlaws of 2020 takes effect.\n",
